 0.02642822265625, 0.0055389404296875, -0.0028896331787109375, 0.005931854248046875, 0.04022216796875, 0.0057830810546875, 0.024658203125, -0.0350341796875, -0.025299072265625, 0.00457000732421875, 0.00836181640625, -0.006130218505859375, -0.0166015625, 0.023529052734375, -0.00647735595703125, 0.000957489013671875, -0.014556884765625, 0.00055694580078125, 0.017608642578125, 0.0323486328125, -0.005523681640625, -0.01340484619140625, 0.0160675048828125, 0.0015058517456054688, 0.0253753662109375, -0.001125335693359375, 0.01343536376953125, -0.0019512176513671875, -0.01390838623046875, 0.015960693359375, 0.004344940185546875, 0.01611328125, -0.011444091796875, 0.01715087890625, 0.039947509765625, -0.0305938720703125, -0.0128326416015625, 0.005908966064453125, 0.004779815673828125, 0.00830841064453125, 0.020721435546875, 0.0084686279296875, -0.0189666748046875, 0.003345489501953125, -0.01125335693359375, -0.0084686279296875, 0.01403045654296875, -0.0283966064453125, 0.0041961669921875, -0.066162109375, 0.005069732666015625, -0.0048675537109375, -0.01457977294921875, 0.0031261444091796875, -0.032989501953125, -0.0093994140625, -0.016265869140625], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "Memory-based self-improving agents are systems that systematically enhance their problem-solving abilities by learning incrementally from successive streams of tasks. These agents persistently store and update natural language memories\u2014summaries and feedback after each episode\u2014which they can later consult to improve future performance. Such methods have demonstrated strong results across a variety of domains, including robotics, virtual assistants, and reasoning tasks, where adaptability and cumulative learning are crucial (see Bengio et al., 2009; Wang et al., 2023; Lee et al., 2022). However, while much attention has focused on mean performance, an important open question is the *reliability* of these agents: specifically, the *consistency* (similarity of performance across repeated runs with unchanged settings) and *robustness* (stability when facing different randomizations or task orderings) of their improvements. These reliability issues are closely related to agent *fragility*\u2014the tendency for small variations, such as early mistakes or task sequence changes, to lead to substantial fluctuations or breakdowns in performance.\n\n**Related Work and Operational Definitions**\n\nPrior work on continual and curriculum learning (Bengio et al., 2009) has emphasized the benefits of orderings where easier tasks precede harder ones, but often neglects analysis of statistical stability. In this study, we focus explicitly on how memory-based agents handle diverse task sequences and how their memory mechanisms may introduce new *failure modes*\u2014patterns or classes of errors that persist or repeat due to breakdowns in memory usage or feedback loops (cf. Wang et al., 2023).\n\nWe use the following operational concepts to guide our analyses:\n- *Consistency*: Minimal variation in measured outcomes (e.g., completion rates, average rewards) across repeated runs with identical settings.\n- *Robustness*: The maintenance of agent performance in the face of external variation, such as task sequence randomization or stochastic initializations.\n- *Fragility*: The degree to which performance deteriorates or becomes unstable due to seemingly minor perturbations, such as a single early failure or change in feedback granularity.\n- *Implicit curriculum*: Unintentional patterns where easy tasks precede harder ones, as opposed to shuffled orderings.\n- *Memory drift*: The gradual buildup of incorrect, ambiguous, or irrelevant memory entries, degrading future behaviors; typically revealed by repeated mistakes or deviation from task goals.\n- *Failure modes*: Systematic breakdowns in performance, often traceable to issues such as ambiguous memory content, misattributed feedback, or poor retrieval dynamics.\n- *Granular feedback*: Structuring feedback at the subtask or finer event granularity, rather than only providing high-level binary success/failure, thereby enabling more precise diagnosis and learning.\n\n**Research Questions**\n\nWe focus on three major research questions:\n1. To what extent do memory-based agents exhibit consistent performance across repeated runs or varied task orderings?\n2. What are the underlying drivers and patterns of fragility or instability (failure modes) in agents\u2019 improvements over time?\n3. Can interventions in memory or feedback structuring (e.g., rubrics or more granular feedback) effectively reduce such weaknesses, and what limitations remain?\n\n**Experimental Setup and Environments**\n\nWe examine two established memory-based agent architectures, each differing in their approach to experience storage and retrieval:\n\n| Agent Type                        | Memory Content & Storage                                     | Memory Usage                                       |\n|------------------------------------|-------------------------------------------------------------|----------------------------------------------------|\n| *Textual episodic memory agent*    | Stores natural-language summaries of actions, errors, corrections after each episode, appended sequentially to persistent memory. | Summaries are retrieved as a reference when facing new tasks; e.g., after an error, the agent consults past mistake summaries.      |\n| *RL agent with memory retrieval*   | Logs structured episode traces: state descriptions, actions, rewards, and feedback, all in text form.               | Prior to acting, retrieves top-k most similar past episodes (measured via embedding-based similarity with current task context), which inform action selection via a Transformer. |\n\nFor the *textual episodic memory agent* (see [Wang et al., 2023]), memory entries are formed from action logs and templated scripts, resulting in concise, human-readable summaries\u2014for example: \"Moved red block to blue square, but forgot to ring bell\u2014try ringing immediately after.\" In contrast, the *RL agent with memory retrieval* ([Lee et al., 2022]) stores more granular structural information for each episode, later retrieving those judged relevant through embedding distances.\n\nBoth agents use a 6-layer Transformer (512 hidden units per layer, 8 attention heads, GELU activations). These architectural choices were made based on previous studies demonstrating effective scaling and stability for agents in comparable task regimes (see Wang et al., 2023; Lee et al., 2022). Hyperparameter selection (including learning rates, memory retrieval k=5, etc.) was tuned for stable early learning and to match the computational budget; full details are in Supplement, but key points include using transformers able to generalize over instruction syntax and a training regime avoiding catastrophic forgetting.\n\nThe memory size is limited to the most recent 100 episodes (sliding window) to balance computational efficiency and recency; preliminary experiments showed that smaller windows led to loss of crucial context, while substantially larger windows induced increased retrieval costs and overfitting to outdated strategies.\n\nWe run 200 episodes per agent and configuration, using 10 random seeds (n=2000 per configuration). The number of seeds is chosen to ensure statistical power for comparisons across task orderings and intervention conditions, as pilot runs indicated high run-to-run variance. All experiments control for environmental stochasticity by using identical task generation seeds for each run, except when explicitly varying task order or randomization. All code, environments, and logs are included in the Supplement, and a summary of training/validation splits, parameter counts, and episode structures is included in Table S2 (reproduced here: agents each process \u223c50K steps per configuration, across evenly stratified task difficulty splits).\n\n**Task Environments**\n\nWe test agents in two environment types:\n- *Procedural tasks*: Agents operate in a 5\u00d75 grid-world, following instructions composed with a 50-word verb/object vocabulary. Tasks increase in complexity with multi-step instructions and more objects (\"Move the green cup onto the table, then press the blue button after placing the red block\").\n- *Reasoning tasks*: Multi-step math or logic problems (\"If you start with 4 coins, double them, give away 3, and add 2, how many left?\"). Difficulty is scaled by number and complexity of operations and distractor elements.\n\nTask generation employs parameterized templates, pulling from a broad input space for each of three difficulty levels (easy: single-step, medium: 2\u20133 steps, hard: 4+ steps). Vocabulary and subtask variety are designed to test generalization (see Supplement Table S1 for full enumeration).\n\n**Evaluation Metrics and Analysis**\n\nMain quantitative outcomes:\n(i) *Task completion rate*: percent of episodes achieving specified goals (per environment/difficulty);\n(ii) *Average episode reward*: mean sum of subgoal rewards.\n\nWe supplement these with qualitative error analysis, examining agent logs to categorize and illustrate frequent or impactful mistakes.\n\nRuns employ 10 random seeds, with each configuration consisting of 200 episodes, to ensure findings are not artifacts of specific initialization or task samples. Task order is shuffled unless curriculum baselines are otherwise specified.\n\nConfounding variables are controlled by (a) using fixed random seeds for both environment and agent initialization (except in explicitly randomized settings), and (b) ensuring that only agent-related parameters and intervention variables are altered between experimental conditions. Changes in environment or agent parameters outside the intended manipulations are strictly avoided, but some residual variance due to random seed and sampling remains (acknowledged as a limitation discussed below).\n\nFor all main comparisons (orderings, agent types, interventions), we report: Welch\u2019s t-test (corrected for non-normality and unequal variances), 95% confidence intervals, and Cohen\u2019s d effect sizes. Multiple comparisons use Holm-Bonferroni adjustment. Key statistical assumptions (normality, variance homogeneity) were checked via Kolmogorov-Smirnov and Levene\u2019s tests; detailed outputs are in Supplementary Materials, but no violations requiring nonparametric alternatives were detected. P-values are reported exactly for all principal results or as <0.01 when below reporting precision.\n\n**Results**\n\n*Performance Consistency, Robustness, and Task Order Effects*\n\nAgents\u2019 task completion rates show substantial variability stemming from both random initialization and especially task ordering. For procedural tasks, completion rates across seeds range from 55% to 88% (mean 68%, SD 13.7%, 95% CI [66.1%, 69.9%]). For reasoning tasks, rates range from 41% to 73% (mean 56%, SD 11.2%, 95% CI [54.5%, 57.5%]). Performance under randomized task orders is significantly lower than under fixed easy-to-hard curricula (mean completion drop: 10\u201318%, curriculum baselines: 84% procedural/67% reasoning, shuffled means: 68%/56%; confidence interval [8.1%, 19.0%], p<0.01, Cohen's d=1.04 for procedural, 0.87 for reasoning). In about 30% of randomized runs, learning plateaus entirely, contrasting with consistent improvements in curriculum runs.\n\nWith adversarial task orders (e.g., hardest tasks presented first, or frequent distractor interleaving), procedural completion drops further to a mean of 60% (SD 14.1%), 17\u201324% below curriculum baseline (95% CI [14.6%, 21.4%], p=0.002, Cohen\u2019s d=1.21). Reasoning completion drops to a mean of 43% (SD 10.7%), again significantly worse"}}